1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:02,797
And then, frankly, there are lots of just small

2
00:00:02,797 --> 00:00:06,308
things we inadvertently say, you know, we can have, sort of, a

3
00:00:06,308 --> 00:00:09,699
beautiful set of data compiled and we can present evidence

4
00:00:09,699 --> 00:00:10,532
and solutions,

5
00:00:10,532 --> 00:00:13,950
and in just a couple small words, we can screw things up.

6
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:17,942
We often follow, for example, into the trap of naive realism,

7
00:00:17,942 --> 00:00:21,535
which is simply this idea that there's just one reality

8
00:00:21,535 --> 00:00:22,200
out there,

9
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:24,910
and it just happens to be the one that I see.

10
00:00:24,910 --> 00:00:26,944
So if you don't see it, I way you're dumb.

11
00:00:26,944 --> 00:00:29,770
And when we unconsciously operate that way,

12
00:00:29,770 --> 00:00:33,663
we'll say things like, well, since it's so obvious that

13
00:00:33,663 --> 00:00:37,195
this is the case, or since this is so unambiguous,

14
00:00:37,195 --> 00:00:41,300
since it's so clear to everybody, since it's unquestionably

15
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:41,937
the case.

16
00:00:41,937 --> 00:00:46,273
Well, the effect of words, like, unambiguous or so clear or

17
00:00:46,273 --> 00:00:49,060
unquestionable is essentially to say,

18
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:53,204
if you have any questions or doubts or see it any differently,

19
00:00:53,204 --> 00:00:55,680
you are a dummy or you're self-interested.

20
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:58,077
So learning to speak with less certainty,

21
00:00:58,077 --> 00:01:01,957
learning to avoid other certain phrases,

22
00:01:01,957 --> 00:01:04,193
I call them frequency words.

23
00:01:04,193 --> 00:01:06,528
My wife and I still joke, you know,

24
00:01:06,528 --> 00:01:11,199
25 years in, how often we would get distracted from the actual content

25
00:01:11,199 --> 00:01:14,015
of what one or the other of us was saying,

26
00:01:14,015 --> 00:01:17,931
because the person who pointed something out would use the

27
00:01:17,931 --> 00:01:19,373
word never or always.

28
00:01:19,373 --> 00:01:22,397
So, for example, if my wife wanted me to actually

29
00:01:22,397 --> 00:01:25,674
help with the dishes, she was actually quite correct

30
00:01:25,674 --> 00:01:28,907
if she would say, you don't help clean up as often

31
00:01:28,907 --> 00:01:30,425
as you could or should.

32
00:01:30,425 --> 00:01:32,377
That was a correct statement.

33
00:01:32,377 --> 00:01:36,927
But if she would say to me, you never help me with the dishes,

34
00:01:36,927 --> 00:01:40,165
that never would trigger me, and I would get into a frequency

35
00:01:40,165 --> 00:01:42,431
argument with her and say, that's not true.

36
00:01:42,431 --> 00:01:46,843
And I would pull out my little no pen and say no, on Tuesday,

37
00:01:46,843 --> 00:01:48,323
July 30th,

38
00:01:48,323 --> 00:01:51,529
I actually put the pizza dishes in that.

39
00:01:51,529 --> 00:01:55,246
And so we'd get derailed into an argument about never or

40
00:01:55,246 --> 00:01:57,975
always and away from the underlying issue itself

41
00:01:57,975 --> 00:01:58,730
around which,

42
00:01:58,730 --> 00:02:00,522
you know, she or I would be right.

43
00:02:00,522 --> 00:02:03,501
Also saying things, for example, like don't take it personal.

44
00:02:03,501 --> 00:02:07,920
I would submit to listeners that we actually never use that phrase

45
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:11,214
except in situations when we know at some level it's personal.

46
00:02:11,214 --> 00:02:14,190
There's no reason you would say that if that wasn't the case.

47
00:02:14,190 --> 00:02:17,460
And so I think avoiding phrases like it's not personal.

48
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:21,518
And listeners, if they want, can easily find a short piece on

49
00:02:21,518 --> 00:02:25,575
hpr.org that I wrote on trap phrases and words to avoid in a

50
00:02:25,575 --> 00:02:29,430
conversation that speak to all of these, kinds of, examples.

51
00:02:29,430 --> 00:02:32,430


